import { DocsPage, Demo, Code, ApiReference } from "../../shell.rx"
import { ToastHero } from "../../demos/toast/hero.rx"
import { ToastManagerDemo } from "../../demos/toast/manager.rx"
import { ToastUndo } from "../../demos/toast/undo.rx"
import { ToastPromise } from "../../demos/toast/promise.rx"
import { ToastPosition } from "../../demos/toast/position.rx"

export default component Page() {
  <DocsPage title="Toast" subtitle="Short messages that appear without taking focus and go away by themselves.">
    <Demo source="toast/hero"><ToastHero /></Demo>

    <h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
    <p>
      Import the parts and put them together. The provider holds the list of toasts; you render it, with a{" "}
      <code>for</code> over <code>(use toast).toasts</code> inside the viewport:
    </p>
    <Code file="toast/anatomy.rx.txt" />

    <h2 id="platform">What the browser does</h2>
    <p>
      The viewport is an element with <code>popover="manual"</code>, shown for as long as it is mounted. That is
      all the browser contributes, and it is enough to drop a part: the toasts are in the top layer, above the
      rest of the page, without a portal or a <code>z-index</code>.
    </p>
    <p>The rest is the library's:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The list: adding, updating and closing toasts through a manager, and the <code>limit</code> on how many show.</li>
      <li>Timers that close each toast, paused while the pointer is over the viewport, while keyboard focus is in it, and while the window doesn't have focus.</li>
      <li>Swiping a toast away, and closing it with <kbd>Escape</kbd> while focus is in it.</li>
      <li><kbd>F6</kbd> to move focus to the viewport while there are toasts, and <kbd>F6</kbd> again to go back.</li>
      <li>Moving focus to the next toast when the focused one closes.</li>
      <li>A polite live region on the viewport, and an assertive alert for a toast added with <code>priority: "high"</code>.</li>
      <li>Measuring each toast, for the CSS variables that stack them.</li>
      <li>Raising the viewport above a modal dialog that opens after it: a popover shown earlier would be painted under the dialog's backdrop.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        There is no Portal part: the viewport is in the top layer already. There are no Positioner and Arrow
        parts, because toasts anchored to an element aren't implemented. To add toasts from anywhere, use the manager from{" "}
        <code>(use toast).manager</code>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>

    <h3 id="adding">Adding toasts</h3>
    <p>
      Inside the provider, <code>(use toast).manager.add(…)</code> adds a toast and returns its id. It takes a{" "}
      <code>title</code> and a <code>description</code>, which <code>ToastTitle</code> and{" "}
      <code>ToastDescription</code> show when they have no children of their own; a <code>type</code>, any
      string, which the parts carry as <code>data-type</code>; a <code>timeout</code>; a <code>priority</code>;{" "}
      <code>onClose</code> and <code>onRemove</code> callbacks; and <code>data</code>, anything you want to read
      back from <code>t.data</code> when you render it. Adding a toast with the <code>id</code> of one that is
      showing updates that one in place and gives it its full time again, so repeating an action doesn't pile up
      toasts. <code>update(id, changes)</code> and <code>close(id)</code> do what they say; <code>close()</code>{" "}
      with no id closes them all.
    </p>

    <h3 id="manager">Adding toasts from anywhere</h3>
    <p>
      To add toasts from outside the provider, or from plain TypeScript, create a manager with{" "}
      <code>createToastManager()</code> from <code>./bisque/internal/toast.js</code>, keep it in a module of its own,
      and pass it to the provider as <code>toastManager</code>. The provider reads it once.
    </p>
    <Demo source="toast/manager"><ToastManagerDemo /></Demo>

    <h3 id="stacking">Stacking</h3>
    <p>
      How the toasts are laid out is all CSS. The first demo stacks them as a deck that spreads out under the
      pointer, from variables each <code>ToastRoot</code> carries: <code>--toast-index</code> (0 for the newest),{" "}
      <code>--toast-offset-y</code> (the heights of the toasts in front of it) and <code>--toast-height</code>,
      with the viewport's <code>--toast-frontmost-height</code>. <code>data-expanded</code> is on the toasts while
      the pointer is over the viewport or keyboard focus is in it, and <code>ToastContent</code> has{" "}
      <code>data-behind</code> on every toast but the front one, to hide their text while the stack is collapsed.
      The other demos are a plain column.
    </p>

    <h3 id="action">An action button</h3>
    <p>
      <code>ToastAction</code> takes its props from the toast's <code>actionProps</code>, with{" "}
      <code>children</code> as its label, and renders nothing when the toast has none. Give a toast with an
      action enough time to be used, or a <code>timeout</code> of <code>0</code> so that it waits to be closed.
    </p>
    <Demo source="toast/undo"><ToastUndo /></Demo>

    <h3 id="promise">Following a promise</h3>
    <p>
      <code>manager.promise(promise, {"{ loading, success, error }"})</code> shows a toast of type{" "}
      <code>"loading"</code>, which never closes by itself, until the promise settles, then turns it into a{" "}
      <code>"success"</code> or <code>"error"</code> toast. Each state is a string, used as the description, or
      the toast's options; <code>success</code> and <code>error</code> may also be functions of the result. It
      returns the promise, which still rejects when it rejects, so catch it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Await it in an action when the action needs the answer: the loading toast still shows at once. Each call
      follows its own promise, so two requests whose answers come back in the other order each end their own
      toast. Give <code>loading</code> an <code>id</code> to have a retry reuse the toast: the demo's failed toast
      has a "Try again" button that calls the same action, and the toast turns back to loading in place instead of
      a second one appearing. (Every other publish fails here, so the retry goes through.)
    </p>
    <Demo source="toast/promise"><ToastPromise /></Demo>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        What you tell the manager lands a moment later, in a change of its own, not in the run of the handler or
        action that called it. An action holds all of its writes until it ends, so a toast added inside one would
        otherwise wait for the action's request. The same goes for a toast's <code>onClose</code> and{" "}
        <code>onRemove</code>: one that is an action awaiting a request doesn't hold the toast's exit.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3 id="position">Position and swiping</h3>
    <p>
      The viewport is a popover, so it comes with the browser's popover styles, centred and bordered: give it a
      position, and <code>pointer-events: none</code> if it covers part of the page while empty. A toast is
      swiped away down or right by default; set <code>swipeDirection</code> to match where the toasts are, and{" "}
      <code>swipeThreshold</code> for how far a swipe must go. A swipe that starts on a button in the toast is a
      press, not a swipe. While dragged, the toast carries <code>data-swiping</code> and{" "}
      <code>--toast-swipe-movement-x</code> and <code>-y</code>; once swiped away, <code>data-swipe-direction</code>{" "}
      says which way it went.
    </p>
    <Demo source="toast/position"><ToastPosition /></Demo>

    <h3 id="timing">Timing and the limit</h3>
    <p>
      A toast closes after the provider's <code>timeout</code> (5 seconds unless you say otherwise), or its own;{" "}
      <code>0</code> means never. The time pauses while the stack is expanded, while the toast is being swiped
      and while the window doesn't have focus, and carries on from where it stopped. Beyond the provider's{" "}
      <code>limit</code> (3), older toasts stay in the list but are inert and carry <code>data-limited</code>, for
      you to hide; they come back as the ones in front close.
    </p>

    <h3 id="animation">Animating them</h3>
    <p>
      Animate a toast in with <code>@starting-style</code>, and out under <code>[data-ending-style]</code>: a
      closing toast stays in the list until the transitions and animations on it and inside it have finished.
      An animation that never ends, such as a spinner, keeps it there, so stop it under{" "}
      <code>[data-ending-style]</code> as the promise demo does.
    </p>

    <h3 id="dialogs">Toasts and modal dialogs</h3>
    <p>
      Toasts stay visible over a modal dialog, including one that opened after them, but everything outside a
      modal dialog is inert: a toast shown over one can be seen and not pressed until the dialog closes.
    </p>

    <h2 id="api-reference">API reference</h2>
    <ApiReference family="toast" />
  </DocsPage>
}
